using NUnit.Framework;
using ArrayList;

[TestFixture]
public class ArrayListContractTest
{
    [PexMethod]
    public void PUT_AddContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList,int x)
    {
        int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        arrList.Add(x);

        int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
        int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
        int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
        bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

        Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount <= New_arrListCount);
        Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX);
        Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
        Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

        // this is to validate the PexAssumeUnderTest attribute;
        // it is not being used for the behavior to be tested.
        Assert.IsTrue(true);
    }
}

Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListCount <= New_arrListCount);
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX);
Assert.IsTrue(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);

In some cases (for example, simple array lists), these tests pass flawlessly. However, for complex list structures (for example, generic list of complex objects), these tests fail with 

`Exception of type 'Microsoft.Pex.Framework.Exceptions.PexAssertFailedException' occurred: Assert failed:`

I believe that the issue is within the way my tests are structured. There could be some issue with the way complex arrays are being tested. Can you suggest a way to better structure these tests?


A: The issue could be due to complex types (like your custom class) not implementing IComparable interface (needed for ordering of different instances, which is a prerequisite for Pex to use these types as parameters). I would suggest to implement the IComparable interface for any complex type you may use in your list.

Another point could be that the ToString method of your complex object might not return readable strings which Pex is using to display tests' results. Pex relies on the ToString method to display what you provided in "...valueForViewing(\"\",...)". If you return unreadable strings from it, you'll see nothing in your tests results, which might be confusing.

Also, note that Pex can create a lot of permutations of parameters and preconditions, so it might fail in some cases if your assertions are too strict. You should design your assertions to be flexible and forgiving.

Lastly, there's an open issue about failing the Pex tests with complex types in the code under test. Check that site as well: https://github.com/Microsoft/Pex/issues

Hope this can help :) `

P.S. Last two points come up because of my experience. In complex scenarios such as these, it's often necessary to design and implement specific interfaces for your classes to work as intended with Pex's test scenarios. It would be great if Microsoft improves these issues so that we don't need to do this kind of programming.--------------------	    // and @@@ index of x in new array is >= 0@@@
